        "speakerGender": "female",
        "transcript": "Today I want to talk about nocturnality, that is, why some animals restrict their activity to the hours of darkness. We'll look at three drivers: temperature, predation, and competition.\n\nStart with temperature. In desert environments, daytime surface heat is simply lethal for small bodies. A kangaroo rat has a high surface-to-volume ratio, so it loses water fast. By staying in a sealed burrow until dusk, it cuts water loss dramatically. In fact, many desert rodents never drink at all. They get their water from metabolizing dry seeds, and that only works if evaporation stays low, which means staying underground while the sun is up.\n\nSecond, predation. If your main predators hunt by sight, darkness is cover. But notice this cuts both ways, because owls and many snakes hunt at night precisely because their prey moved there.\n\nThird, competition. Here's the point students most often get wrong. People assume nocturnal animals were always nocturnal, that it's some fixed ancestral trait. Often it isn't. It's a schedule negotiated with neighbors. Take the coyote. In wilderness areas coyotes are active across the whole day. Move them near a city, and the same species shifts to almost entirely night activity. Nothing about their eyes changed. What changed was human traffic during daylight.\n\nSo the lesson is that activity timing is flexible, a behavioral response to current pressure rather than a locked-in feature of the species. When you analyze any nocturnal population, ask what it's avoiding, and ask whether that avoidance is recent.",
